            "content": "# PRD: WebSocket PTY Steering for execd\n\n**Status:** Ready for re-implementation\n**Source:** Analyzed from git history `381bcf8..6d32a7b` (tty-fork branch, to be dropped)\n\n---\n\n## 1. Background\n\n`execd` is a sandboxed code-execution daemon. Before this feature, the only way to interact with a bash session was via the fire-and-forget `POST /session/:id/run` SSE endpoint. That model does not support:\n\n- Interactive programs (shells, REPLs, vim, htop) that expect a terminal\n- Real-time stdin injection mid-execution\n- Cursor-control / ANSI escape sequences (PTY echo, colors, progress bars)\n- Persistent shell state between two WS connections (reconnect)\n\n## 2. Goals\n\n| # | Goal |\n|---|------|\n| G1 | Provide a persistent, bidirectional WebSocket channel per bash session for stdin/stdout/stderr steering |\n| G2 | Support both **pipe mode** (separate stdout/stderr pipes, text-only) and **PTY mode** (full terminal emulation via `/dev/ptmx`) selectable at connection time |\n| G3 | Allow clients to **reconnect** to a live session and replay any output missed while disconnected |\n| G4 | Keep existing `POST /session/:id/run` (SSE fire-and-forget) fully operational alongside the WS channel |\n| G5 | No regressions on Windows build (Windows stubs for all new symbols) |\n\n## 3. Non-Goals\n\n- Client-side terminal emulator (xterm.js) — out of scope for execd\n- Multi-client fan-out (only one WS connection per session at a time)\n- Persistent session storage across daemon restarts\n\n## 4. User Stories\n\n**US1 – Interactive shell**\nAs an agent, I want to connect a WebSocket to a bash session and interactively type commands, so that I can run programs that require terminal input (e.g. a Python REPL, `vim`, `ssh`).\n\n**US2 – PTY terminal (full ANSI)**\nAs a client, I want to opt into PTY mode (`?pty=1`) so I receive raw terminal bytes including ANSI escape codes, cursor movements, and color — equivalent to running bash inside a real terminal.\n\n**US3 – Terminal resize**\nAs a client in PTY mode, I want to send a `resize` frame to update the PTY window size, so programs that query `COLUMNS`/`LINES` (e.g. `htop`, `man`) lay out correctly.\n\n**US4 – Signal injection**\nAs a client, I want to send a `signal` frame (e.g. `SIGINT`) to kill or interrupt the running process inside the session, so I can cancel a runaway command.\n\n**US5 – Reconnect with replay**\nAs a client that temporarily loses network connectivity, I want to reconnect and supply `?since=<offset>` to retrieve all output I missed, so I do not have to restart the session.\n\n**US6 – Coexistence with fire-and-forget**\nAs an agent, I want to continue using `POST /session/:id/run` (SSE) to run discrete commands in the same bash session, with all output stored in the replay buffer, so the two modes interoperate.\n\n## 5. Functional Requirements\n\n### 5.1 REST Session Lifecycle\n\n| Method | Endpoint | Description |\n|--------|----------|-------------|\n| POST | `/session` | Create a bash session; returns `session_id` |\n| GET | `/session/:sessionId` | Get session status + current replay buffer offset |\n| POST | `/session/:sessionId/run` | Run code in session (SSE stream); supports `?since=<offset>` for missed-output replay |\n| DELETE | `/session/:sessionId` | Terminate and delete session |\n\n### 5.2 WebSocket Endpoint\n\n`GET /ws/session/:sessionId[?pty=1][&since=<offset>]`\n\n- HTTP 404 returned **before** upgrade if session does not exist\n- HTTP 409 returned if another WS client is already connected\n- Upgrade HTTP → WebSocket (RFC 6455)\n- `?pty=1` — start bash via PTY; omit for pipe mode\n- `?since=<offset>` — replay missed output from that byte offset on first connect\n\n### 5.3 WebSocket Framing Protocol\n\n**Client → Server frames (JSON):**\n\n| `type` | Fields | Action |\n|--------|--------|--------|\n| `stdin` | `data: string` | Write bytes to bash stdin |\n| `signal` | `signal: string` | Send named signal to process group (SIGINT, SIGTERM, SIGKILL, SIGQUIT, SIGHUP) |\n| `resize` | `cols: int`, `rows: int` | Resize PTY window (PTY mode only; silently ignored in pipe mode) |\n| `ping` | — | Application-level keepalive request |\n\n**Server → Client frames (JSON):**\n\n| `type` | Fields | Meaning |\n|--------|--------|---------|\n| `connected` | `session_id`, `mode: \"pipe\"\\|\"pty\"` | First frame after upgrade; confirms mode |\n| `replay` | `data: string`, `offset: int64` | Missed output since requested offset |\n| `stdout` | `data: string`, `timestamp: int64` | Live stdout chunk |\n| `stderr` | `data: string`, `timestamp: int64` | Live stderr chunk (pipe mode only) |\n| `exit` | `exit_code: int` | Process exited |\n| `pong` | — | Response to application-level ping |\n| `error` | `error: string`, `code: string` | Error codes: `SESSION_GONE`, `START_FAILED`, `STDIN_WRITE_FAILED`, `INVALID_FRAME`, `ALREADY_CONNECTED`, `RUNTIME_ERROR` |\n\n### 5.4 Keepalive\n\n- RFC 6455 binary ping every 30 s; 60 s read deadline reset on pong\n- Application-level `ping`/`pong` frames supported in parallel\n\n### 5.5 Reconnect Semantics\n\n1. Client disconnects (network drop or `conn.Close()`)\n2. Bash process stays alive; output continues to accumulate in replay buffer\n3. Client reconnects with `?since=<last_offset>`\n4. Server sends a `replay` frame with all missed bytes, then streams live output\n\n### 5.6 Output Delivery Guarantee\n\n- Output is **written to the replay buffer first**, then fanned out to the active WS connection\n- A client that disconnects and reconnects receives each byte **exactly once** (no duplicates, no gaps)\n- Replay buffer capacity: **1 MiB** (circular); oldest bytes are evicted when full\n\n### 5.7 Output Format\n\n- Chunks are raw bytes (not line-buffered) so PTY control sequences and prompts are delivered immediately without waiting for `\\n`\n- Chunk size: up to **32 KiB** per read\n\n## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n\n| Category | Requirement |\n|----------|-------------|\n| Concurrency | Race-free under `-race`; all shared state behind mutexes or atomics |\n| Goroutine lifecycle | No goroutine leaks — pump goroutines must exit before WS lock is released on disconnect |\n| File descriptor lifecycle | All `os.Pipe` read/write ends closed when no longer needed |\n| Windows build | Stub implementations for all new `Controller` methods and `BashSession` interface |\n| Test coverage | Unit tests for replay buffer; integration tests for WS connect/reconnect/exit/PTY |\n\n## 7. Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `GET /ws/session/:id` upgrades to WS and delivers `connected` frame\n- [ ] stdin typed in the client appears as output from bash\n- [ ] `?pty=1` produces ANSI escape sequences in stdout frames\n- [ ] `resize` frame changes PTY dimensions (verified via `stty size` inside bash)\n- [ ] `signal` SIGINT interrupts a running command\n- [ ] Disconnecting and reconnecting with `?since=0` delivers all prior output exactly once\n- [ ] Concurrent `POST /session/:id/run` calls while WS is connected work correctly\n- [ ] `go test -race ./...` passes with no data-race reports\n- [ ] Windows build succeeds (`GOOS=windows go build ./...`)\n",
